        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Choosing And Typing"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Class-based when you need named per-function state",
            "Add `__get__` if that class decorates methods",
            "Use `ParamSpec` so typed callers keep their signatures"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "7. Argument-Taking Template",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "def repeat(n):        # factory",
            "  def deco(fn):       # decorator",
            "    @wraps(fn)",
            "    def w(*a, **kw):  # wrapper",
            "      return fn(*a, **kw)",
            "    return w",
            "  return deco"
          ],
          "note": "call it @repeat(3), never bare @repeat"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Where The Line Sits",
          "text": "Decorators carry cross-cutting concerns: logging, caching, retry, auth, timing, registration. Business logic stays inside the function."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "caption": {
    "text": "Part 3 of PYTHON HANDBOOK. Every Python decorator you have ever written is one line of sugar over `f = deco(f)` — and every weird bug in one traces back to a closure cell you never looked inside. This handbook goes from first-class functions and LEGB scope through wraps, argument forwarding, factories, class-based decorators and stacking order, then lands on the patterns you actually ship: cache, retry, timing, auth, registration. Ten pages, one save, the whole wrapping model.",
